  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Germination is the process by which a seed begins to grow and develop
    into a new plant. A seed contains a tiny young plant called an embryo, along
    with stored food that provides energy during the early stages of growth. For
    germination to begin, the seed needs suitable conditions such as water, oxygen,
    and an appropriate temperature.\nIn a bean plant, germination begins when the
    bean seed absorbs water from the soil. The seed swells, and its outer covering,
    called the seed coat, begins to break open. The first structure to emerge is
    the radicle, which develops into the primary root. It grows downward into the
    soil and begins absorbing water and minerals.\nNext, the young shoot grows upward
    toward the surface. As it emerges above the soil, its leaves unfold and begin
    receiving sunlight. At this stage, the young plant is called a seedling. Initially,
    stored food inside the seed supports its development, but as the leaves grow,
    the plant becomes increasingly able to produce its own food through photosynthesis.\nThe
    roots continue to spread through the soil while the stem becomes taller and stronger.
    With sufficient sunlight, water, air, and nutrients, the seedling gradually develops
    into a mature bean plant.\nAs the plant reaches maturity, flower buds develop
    and bloom. These flowers are important for the plant's reproductive cycle and
    can eventually contribute to the formation of new seeds.\nThus, germination transforms
    a small, inactive seed into a growing plant through a sequence of stages: seed
    \u2192 swelling \u2192 root \u2192 shoot \u2192 seedling \u2192 growing plant
    \u2192 mature plant."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
